Hi, I am currently a research assistant at the Senckenberg Society for Nature Research, working group Benthic Biogeography. I earned my B.Sc. and M.Sc. degrees in Biology at the University of Hamburg, and have found my to the deep-sea biology research. I focus on the systematics, taxonomy, biogeography and ecology of the peracarid crustacean, particularly the asellote isopods. 



--


Zhehao studied for a B.Sc. and an M.Sc. in Biology at the University of Hamburg. He began his research career in deep-sea biology with an integrative taxonomy project on the deep-sea isopod genus _Acanthocope_ under the supervision of Prof. Dr. Saskia Brix. The project has resulted in the discovery of a new species. Following the project, he had the opportunity to participate in an international expedition with Ocean Census and the Schmidt Ocean Institute heading to the Southern Ocean, with primary focus on discovering unknown species. During the subsequent workshop, Zhehao identified and described two new isopod species in collaboration with a researcher from the University of Buenos Aires.

Returning from the Southern Hemisphere, Zhehao was able to utilise the accquired expertise to support two further species discovery workshops in Germany. These workshops led to the joint discovery of five species and a genus, all new to science.

Zhehao further developed his taxonomic expertise by expanding his focus to include systematics and biogeography. In his master's thesis, he employed an integrative approach to describe two new species, and to study the biogeography on another deep-sea isopod family Haploniscidae, which contrasts the _Acanthocope_ in terms of locomotion style. His findings highlighted the complex interplay between an animal's life style, morphology, and geographic features in shaping the distribution patterns of deep-sea benthic isopods.

During his Master's program, Zhehao attended four research conferences, where he presented his studies and established connections with the international research communities. Gradually becoming more involved in the deep-sea benthic biology research, Zhehao has started his first position as postgraduate researcher at Senckenberg Gesellschaft für Naturforschung in October, bringing his expertise in taxonomy, molecular techniques, and statistical analysis to various projects. From analyzing legacy sampling campaigns such as BIOICE, to participating in ongoing programs such as ALONGate project, and evaluating Deep Arctic expedition by Greenpeace. Meanwhile, Zhehao is preparing his PhD project, through which he aims to further deepen our understanding of the diversity and evolution of the isopods and other peracarid crustaceans living in the remote and poorly studied deep-sea environments.

